## Chapter 6 Results

In this chapter the main results of the analysis of \(J/\psi\) in 0 - 5 % central U+U collisions at \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}=~{}193\) GeV are presented, particularly \(J/\psi\) invariant yield and nuclear modification factor.

### 6.1 Invariant yield

To obtain the invariant yield of \(J/\psi\) in 0 - 5 % central U+U collisions the \(J/\psi\) raw yield was corrected on the total reconstruction efficiency (Chapter 4) and normalized to the phase space and the number of events analyzed. The invariant \(J/\psi\)\(p_{\rm T}\) spectrum is defined as

\[B\frac{d^{2}N}{d\phi p_{\rm T}dp_{\rm T}dy}=\frac{1}{2\pi p_{\rm T}\Delta p_{ \rm T}\Delta y}\frac{N_{J/\psi}}{\epsilon_{\rm total}}\frac{1}{N_{\rm ev}} \tag{6.1}\]

where \(B\) denotes the branching ratio \(J/\psi\to e^{+}e^{-}\), \(p_{\rm T}\) is the the mean transverse momentum in a bin of width \(\Delta p_{\rm T}\) and the rapidity interval is \(\Delta y=2\) for \(|y|<1\). \(N_{J/\psi}\) is the \(J/\psi\) raw yield in a given \(p_{\rm T}\) bin (Chapter 3), \(\epsilon_{\rm total}\) is the total \(J/\psi\) reconstruction efficiency discussed in Section 4.3 and \(N_{\rm ev}\) is the number of events analyzed satisfying the event cuts (ca. 56 M). Figure 6.1 shows the resulting \(J/\psi\) invariant yield as a function of the weighted average of \(p_{\rm T}\) in given bin. Statistical and systematic uncertainties are also depicted. The yield decreases towards higher \(p_{\rm T}\) and for the highest \(p_{\rm T}\) the decrease is the most significant.

### 6.2 Nuclear modification factor

Nuclear modification factor of \(J/\psi\) as a function of \(p_{\rm T}\) was calculated according to

\[R_{\rm AA}(p_{\rm T},y)=\frac{1}{<N_{bin}>0.964/\sigma_{\rm inel}^{\rm pp}} \frac{d^{2}N_{\rm AA}/dp_{\rm T}dy}{d^{2}\sigma_{\rm pp}/dp_{\rm T}dy} \tag{6.2}\]

where \(d^{2}N_{\rm AA}/dp_{\rm T}dy\) denotes \(J/\psi\) invariant yield in U+U collisions defined above, \(<N_{\rm bin}>\) is the average number of binary nucleon-nucleon collisions in 0-5% central U+U collisions (see Chapter 4). \(\sigma_{\rm inel}^{\rm pp}\) is p+p inelastic cross section and U+U collisions 193 GeV and \(d^{2}\sigma_{\rm pp}/dp_{\rm T}dy\) is the invariant cross section of \(J/\psi\) production in p+p collisions. Thescaling factor 0.964 was included to account for the difference between the charm cross section in p+p collisions at 200 GeV and 193 GeV.

Since STAR lacks \(J/\psi\) low-\(p_{\rm T}\) data in p+p collisions, and to make the results of this \(J/\psi\) analysis consistent with those from minimum bias U+U collisions, the same p+p reference was used, specifically the data from PHENIX [58] (for \(p_{\rm T}~{}<~{}2\) GeV/\(c\)) and STAR [49] (for \(p_{\rm T}~{}>~{}2\) GeV/\(c\)). However, the PHENIX data are only for \(|y|<0.35\) so it was assumed that the production of \(J/\psi\) in that narrower rapidity bin is same as in \(|y|<1\). The p+p reference fitted with the function 4.3 is shown in the Figure 6.2.

The fitting function was evaluated in the average \(p_{\rm T}\) of each \(J/\psi\)\(p_{\rm T}\) bin and this value

